[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     => <x,x'> - (||x||² + ||x'||² + 2||y||² - d²)/2 + ||y||² = 0
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     => <x,x'> - (||x||² + ||x'||²)/2 - (2||y||² - d²)/2 + ||y||² = 0
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     => <x,x'> - (||x||² + ||x'||²)/2 - ||y||² + d²/2 + ||y||² = 0
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     => <x,x'> - (||x||² + ||x'||²)/2 + d²/2 = 0
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     => <x,x'> = (||x||² + ||x'||² - d²)/2   ...(10)
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 25. But this is exactly what we have from the distance condition! So the orthogonality condition is automatically satisfied if we can find y such that ||x-y||² = d²/2 for all x in S. The key is to find a point y that is equidistant from all points in S with distance d/√2.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 26. So the problem reduces to: Given a set S in a Hilbert space with ||x-x'|| = d for all x≠x', show that there exists a point y such that ||x-y|| = d/√2 for all x in S.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 27. How to find such a y? Consider the function F(z) = sup_{x in S} | ||x-z||² - d²/2 |. We want to minimize this to zero. But this is not constructive.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 28. Alternatively, take any two points x1, x2 in S. The set of points equidistant from x1 and x2 is a hyperplane. The condition ||z-x1|| = ||z-x2|| = d/√2 means that z lies on the intersection of two spheres. The midpoint of x1 and x2 is (x1+x2)/2, and the distance from this midpoint to either x1 is d/2. We want ||z-x1|| = d/√2. So by the Pythagorean theorem, the distance from z to the midpoint should be sqrt( (d/√2)^2 - (d/2)^2 ) = sqrt( d²/2 - d²/4 ) = d/2.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]     So for two points, the set of centers y is a sphere around the midpoint with radius d/2.
