[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] **Core Principle Identification**:
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 1. For part (1): The core principle is about combining multiple sparse sets into one sparse oracle set. The key insight is that we can encode information about membership in S₁, S₂, ..., Sₖ for strings of length n into a single string of polynomial length. Since each Sᵢ is sparse, the total number of strings across all Sᵢ of length n is bounded by k·p(n), which is still polynomial. We can define S to contain encoded versions of these strings (e.g., ⟨x, i⟩ for x ∈ Sᵢ), and the oracle machine M can query S with an appropriately encoded string to check membership.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 2. For part (2): The core principle relates to the power of computable angel strings. If the angel string is computable in polynomial time, then P_bad-angel is just P itself, because we can compute αₙ in poly-time and then run M(x, αₙ). For NP, since NP ⊆ P_angel (because the angel string can be the witness, but note the problem says it's not similar to NP certificate, but technically it can be used that way), but with computable angel strings, we cannot capture NP unless P=NP, because if we could compute the witness in poly-time, then we could decide NP languages in poly-time.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 3. For part (3): This is similar to part (1) but for a single P_angel language L. The angel string αₙ for length n has polynomial length p(n). We can define a sparse set S_L that contains the pairs ⟨n, αₙ⟩ for each n. Since for each n, there is only one αₙ, the set S_L is sparse (each length has at most one string). Then an oracle machine with access to S_L can, given x of length n, query S_L for αₙ, and then compute M(x, αₙ).
