[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] **Strategy Formulation**: 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 1. Recognize that the expected number of days E is the sum from k=0 to 5 of the expected waiting time to get the (k+1)-th tree, which is geometric with success probability p_k = 1 - (1/2)^(6-k).
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 2. Therefore, E = sum_{k=0}^{5} 1 / [1 - (1/2)^(6-k)].
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 3. Compute this sum: 
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    For k=0: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^6] = 1 / (1 - 1/64) = 1 / (63/64) = 64/63
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    k=1: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^5] = 1 / (1 - 1/32) = 32/31
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    k=2: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^4] = 1 / (1 - 1/16) = 16/15
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    k=3: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^3] = 1 / (1 - 1/8) = 8/7
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    k=4: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^2] = 1 / (1 - 1/4) = 4/3
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122]    k=5: 1 / [1 - (1/2)^1] = 1 / (1 - 1/2) = 2/1 = 2
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 4. So E = 64/63 + 32/31 + 16/15 + 8/7 + 4/3 + 2.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 5. Find a common denominator and sum these fractions to get m/n in lowest terms.
[INFO] 09-21 22:29:36 run_experiments.py:122] 6. Then compute 100m + n.
