[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] **Core Principle Identification**: The problem involves saddle pairs in a matrix, defined by two conditions: (i) for every row, there is a row in R that dominates it on the columns in C, and (ii) for every column, there is a column in C that is dominated by it on the rows in R. A minimal saddle pair is one with no proper subset pair that is also a saddle pair. The core principle is to show that any two minimal saddle pairs have the same number of rows using an injective function argument between their row sets.
[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] **Pitfall Prediction**: 
[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] - *Conceptual Trap*: Misunderstanding the conditions for a saddle pair, particularly the "for all" quantifiers in (i) and (ii). 
[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] - *Completeness Trap*: Failing to use the minimality condition properly to derive a contradiction when assuming non-injective functions.
[INFO] 09-22 03:31:23 run_experiments.py:122] - *Strategy Trap*: Not recognizing that the injectivity of functions between row sets of minimal saddle pairs implies equal cardinality.
