[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] **Strategy Formulation**:
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 1. Apply Leibniz's rule to differentiate f(ν) with respect to ν. This will give us:
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122]    f'(ν) = [integrand at x=1/ν] × d(1/ν)/dν - [integrand at x=1] × d(1)/dν + ∫ ∂(integrand)/∂ν dx
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 2. Simplify the first term: The integrand at x=1/ν involves √((1/ν²-1)(1-ν²(1/ν)²)) = √((1-ν²)/ν²) × √(1-1) = 0. So this term contributes 0.
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 3. The second term is also 0 since the lower limit is constant.
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 4. Calculate ∂(integrand)/∂ν by differentiating 1/√((x²-1)(1-ν²x²)) with respect to ν.
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 5. Simplify the resulting integral and determine its sign for all ν in (0,1).
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 05:15:09 run_experiments.py:122] 6. Conclude that f'(ν) < 0, proving that f(ν) is monotonically decreasing.
