[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] **Core Principle Identification**: This problem involves combinatorial game theory, specifically the concept of nim-values (or Sprague-Grundy theory). The key insight is to identify winning and losing positions in this impartial game. A position is a losing position if the player whose turn it is will lose with optimal play from both sides. A position is a winning position if the player whose turn it is can force a win with optimal play. In impartial games like this, a position is a losing position if and only if no move leads to another losing position.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] **Pitfall Prediction**:
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] - *Conceptual Trap*: Failing to recognize this is an impartial game where positions can be classified as N-positions (winning for the Next player) or P-positions (winning for the Previous player).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] - *Calculation Trap*: Missing some square numbers (1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ...) or miscalculating which positions are P-positions.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] - *Completeness Trap*: Identifying some P-positions but failing to prove there are infinitely many, or failing to prove that these positions are indeed P-positions.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] **Strategy Formulation**:
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 1. First, I'll identify what moves are possible: players can remove 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, etc. pebbles.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 2. I'll analyze small positions to find a pattern of P-positions (positions where the previous player wins, i.e., the second player wins from the initial position).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 3. I'll use the fact that a position is a P-position if and only if all possible moves from it lead to N-positions.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 4. I'll look for a pattern in the P-positions and prove that pattern continues infinitely.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 5. I'll verify that these positions are indeed P-positions by showing that any move from them leads to an N-position.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 6. Finally, I'll conclude that there are infinitely many initial positions where the second player can win.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] </think>
