[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 5. For a prime p, we need (p^(n+1) - 1)/(p - 1) ≡ 1 (mod 43) and (mod 47).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 6. This simplifies to p^(n+1) ≡ p (mod 43) and p^(n+1) ≡ p (mod 47).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 7. Further simplification: p^n ≡ 1 (mod 43) and p^n ≡ 1 (mod 47).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 8. By Fermat's Little Theorem, p^(43-1) ≡ 1 (mod 43) and p^(47-1) ≡ 1 (mod 47) for any prime p not dividing 43 or 47.
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 9. So n must be divisible by both 42 and 46, or by lcm(42, 46).
[INFO] 09-22 06:54:53 run_experiments.py:122] 10. Calculate lcm(42, 46), then find the prime factorization of this value.
